.TH MDNSCTL 1 "October 2026" "mdnsd 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
mdnsctl \- query the mdnsd daemon
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mdnsctl
[\fB\-s\fR \fIPATH\fR]
[\fB\-v\fR]
[\fB\-\-json\fR | \fB\-\-segmented\-json\fR]
.I command
[\fIargument\fR ...]
.br
.B mdnsctl
.B \-\-help
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mdnsctl
asks a running
.BR mdnsd (8)
daemon what it knows, over the daemon's control socket.
Each command of the control protocol has a subcommand here.
Options must come before the command; everything after the command name
belongs to the command, which for
.B devices
and
.B watch
includes their own
.B \-\-query
and
.BR \-\-interval .
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B segments
List the configured segments, one line each, with the interfaces in the segment
(an untrusted one marked, as it is announced nothing) and the address families
(v4, v6) it covers.
.TP
\fBdevices\fR [\fB\-\-query\fR \fItype\fR]... [\fB\-\-wait\fR \fIspan\fR] \fIname\fR
List the devices discovered on the interfaces of the segment called
.IR name ,
in both address families.
By default each device is one line: its DNS name (for example
.BR OZAL\-Speakers.local ),
its pretty name, which is the name of its services (for example
.BR "OZAL Speakers" ),
and, last, all of its addresses, IPv4 first, whichever family it was heard
over.
Devices that have not announced a name yet have no DNS name to show, so they
are only counted.
The listing ends with how many devices there are.
With
.B \-v
every device is listed with its interface, addresses of both families (routable
first, then link-local) and hostname (a
hostname of
.B \-
means the device has been heard but has not announced a name), and
.B addr-ttl=
after it: the TTL, in seconds, its address records were announced with, if it
announced any, followed by its
services: instance, type, port, target, the
.B ttl=
its records were announced with,
.B sub=
and the subtypes it is of (such as
.B _universal
for an AirPrint printer), and TXT records.
These are the TTLs a reflected copy is announced with (see
.B ReflectTTL=
in
.BR mdnsd.conf (5)).
.IP
Only devices discovered on the segment's own interfaces are listed.
Devices that the daemon reflected onto those interfaces from other segments are
not.
.IP
The name needs no quoting, even if it contains spaces:
.B mdnsctl devices IoT to br0
works.
.IP
With
.B \-\-query
.I type
(a service type such as
.BR _shelly._tcp ;
.B .local
may be left off, and the option may be given more than once, as
.B \-\-query
.I type
or
.BI \-\-query= type\fR),
only the devices of that type are listed, each with only its services of that
type, and they are asked for by name on every interface of the segment:
.B mdnsctl
watches the segment for the type, as
.B "watch \-\-query"
does, for
.B \-\-wait
.I span
(4 seconds unless given, such as
.B 10s
or
.BR 1.5s ).
Each device is printed as without
.B \-\-query
(and with
.BR \-v )
as soon as it is found, those of the type already known at once and the others
as they answer, and the count once the wait is over.
With
.BR \-\-json ,
nothing is printed until the wait is over, then one reply as without
.BR \-\-query :
each device as last heard of, and none that went in the meantime.
Many devices, smart plugs among them, answer only when asked for their type: a
probe, which asks what types there are, does not find them, and neither does
waiting.
The daemon remembers the type, so later probes ask for it too.
.TP
\fBprobe\fR [\fIname\fR]
Actively look for devices instead of waiting for them to announce themselves:
ask the interfaces of the segment called
.I name
(of every segment if no name is given) which service types exist, browse each
type for instances, resolve every instance that has not yet said where it is,
and ask for the addresses of any named device that has no routable one yet.
This takes three rounds of queries over about four seconds, and the command
returns once the answers have been processed.
It prints how many queries were sent on each interface; use
.B devices
to see what answered.
.IP
The daemon already does this at startup, and again every
.B ProbeInterval=
if one is set (see
.B [Probe]
and
.B [Segment]
in
.BR mdnsd.conf (5)).
Run it to probe right now, for example to pick up a device you have just
switched on.
.TP
\fBwatch\fR [\fB\-\-query\fR \fItype\fR]... [\fB\-\-interval\fR \fIspan\fR] \fIname\fR
Follow the segment called
.I name
and print each device as it appears, changes and goes, until interrupted.
Without
.BR \-\-query ,
nothing is printed for what is already there: use
.B devices
for that.
Each line starts with what happened (\fBadded\fR, \fBupdated\fR or
\fBremoved\fR) and the device's
.BR ttl :
for how many more seconds the daemon keeps it if it does not hear from it again,
or 0 once it is gone, because it said goodbye or ran out.
The rest is as
.B devices
shows it: DNS name, pretty name and addresses.
A device is
.B added
the first time it is shown with a name, and
.B updated
afterwards; devices that have not announced a name are not shown.
With
.B \-v
every change is the device as
.B devices \-v
lists it, services included, and unnamed devices too.
With
.B \-\-json
each change is one line of JSON, unchanged from the daemon, for scripts.
.IP
With
.B \-\-query
.IR type ,
as for
.BR devices ,
only devices of that type are shown, each with only its services of that type:
first each one already known, as
.BR present ,
then the daemon asks for the type, and shows what answers and what changes from
then on.
A device that loses its last service of the type is shown as
.BR removed .
With
.B \-\-interval
.I span
(such as
.B 30s
or
.BR 5min ,
at least ten seconds), the daemon asks again that often for as long as the
watch runs, so that a device that only answers when asked is found soon after it
is switched on.
Without
.BR \-\-interval ,
it asks once.
.IP
It exits with status 2 if the daemon stops or the connection is lost, and
quietly with status 0 if its output is closed, as with
.BR "mdnsctl watch IoT | head" .
.TP
\fBsubscribe\fR [\fB\-\-query\fR \fItype\fR]... [\fB\-\-interval\fR \fIspan\fR] [\fB\-\-foreground\fR] [\fB\-\-log\fR \fIfile\fR] \fIname\fR \fB\-\-\fR \fIscript\fR [\fIarg\fR ...]
Watch the segment called
.I name
as
.B watch
does, with the same
.B \-\-query
and
.BR \-\-interval ,
and run
.I script
with its
.IR arg s
for each change, one at a time, in the order they come.
It is for scripts that run and are gone: the watch is opened, and anything
wrong with it (no daemon, no such segment, a script that is not an executable
file) is reported, before
.B mdnsctl
goes into the background, prints its PID and returns.
The subscriber then runs as the user who started it, with that user's
permissions, until it is killed; the daemon runs nothing.
.IP
The script is run directly, not through a shell, so its first line
.RB ( #!/bin/sh ,
say) says what runs it; a name without a slash is looked up in
.BR PATH .
It gets the change on its standard input, as one line of JSON the same as
.B "watch \-\-json"
prints, and in its environment:
.BR MDNS_EVENT
.RB ( present ,
.BR added ,
.B updated
or
.BR removed ),
.BR MDNS_SEGMENT ,
.BR MDNS_TTL ,
.BR MDNS_INTERFACE ,
.B MDNS_HOSTNAME
and
.B MDNS_ADDRESS
(the device's first address), each empty when the change has none.
A script that fails is logged, and the next change runs it again.
Changes that come while it runs wait for it, up to 1024 of them; one more is
dropped, and logged.
.IP
If the daemon goes away (it is restarted, say), the subscriber watches again
once it is back, trying after a second and then less and less often, up to
every 30 seconds; with
.BR \-\-query ,
the devices of the type already known come again as
.BR present .
.B mdnsctl watchers
lists a subscriber, and
.B kill
with its PID stops it, and a script it is running then.
.IP
.B \-\-log
.I file
appends what the subscriber has to say (when it starts, stops, loses the
daemon, or a script fails) and the script's output to
.IR file ;
without it they are discarded.
.B \-\-foreground
keeps it attached instead, with both on the terminal, for running it under a
supervisor or trying a script out.
.TP
.B watchers
List the watches open on the daemon, oldest first, one line each: its id, the
segment it watches, its
.B \-\-query
types and
.B \-\-interval
.RB ( \-
for none), how long it has been open, and the process holding it: its PID, its
user and its command line.
The daemon has the PID and user from the kernel, as the other end of the
watch's connection, so a watcher cannot misreport them.
The user name and command line are looked up by
.B mdnsctl
itself: a user that is not in
.I /etc/passwd
is shown by number, and the command line of a process this one may not see (as
with
.B hidepid
on
.IR /proc )
as
.BR \- .
.TP
.B timers
List the timers the daemon has scheduled, soonest first, with their state,
when each is due, how often it has run, when it last ran and how long that
took.
Each interface has one
.B upkeep
timer, which drops devices as they expire, asks the ones about to expire to
answer again, and re-announces reflected devices; it is set for the earliest of
those that is due, so this shows when the daemon will next do any of them.
Under each timer is everything it is waiting for, soonest first, which is what
it is set to the first of:
.B expire
a device that has not been heard from, then
.B refresh
it (ask it to answer again, shortly before that), and
.B re-announce
a name the daemon announces.
A device that has no name yet is listed by the address it was heard from.
.IP
A state of
.B running
means its callback is running now,
.B waiting
that it is due at the time shown (\fBnow\fR if it is overdue behind another
callback), and
.B idle
that nothing is due.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-s\fR, \fB\-\-socket\fR \fIPATH\fR
Use the control socket at
.I PATH
instead of the default,
.IR /run/mdnsd/mdnsd.sock .
.B \-\-socket=\fIPATH\fR
is also accepted.
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
List devices with everything the daemon knows: interface, addresses, the TTLs they were announced with, and every service with its port,
target and TXT records, including devices that have not announced a name.
Only
.B devices
and
.B watch
are affected.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print the daemon's reply unchanged: one line of JSON, suitable for
.BR jq (1).
.TP
.B \-\-segmented\-json
For
.B devices
only: print each device as a line of JSON of its own, as it is in the
.B devices
array of
.BR \-\-json ,
and nothing else.
With
.BR \-\-query ,
each is printed the moment it is first found, as the text listing is, and not
again when it changes.
It cannot be given with
.BR \-\-json .
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Show usage and exit.
.SH OUTPUT
Without
.BR \-\-json ,
.B segments
prints a table:
.PP
.nf
.RS
NAME         INTERFACES             FAMILIES
media_stuff  br0, media             v4, v6
IoT          wifi                   v4, v6
IoT to br0   br0, wifi (untrusted)  v4, v6
.RE
.fi
.PP
and
.B devices
prints one line per named device: its DNS name, its pretty name and its addresses:
.PP
.nf
.RS
  printer.local  Printer  10.0.0.10
IoT to br0: 1 device
.RE
.fi
.PP
With
.BR \-v ,
it lists everything, including addresses and service details:
.PP
.nf
.RS
  br0   10.0.0.10, fd00::10  printer.local  addr-ttl=120
      Printer  _http._tcp.local  :631 -> printer.local  ttl=4500  [rp=ipp, ty=hp]
  wifi  10.0.1.7             \-
IoT to br0: 2 devices
.RE
.fi
.PP
and
.B probe
prints what it sent where:
.PP
.nf
.RS
INTERFACE  RESULT
br0        14 queries sent
wifi       9 queries sent
.RE
.fi
.PP
.B watch
prints one line per change:
.PP
.nf
.RS
added    ttl=4500   plug.local  Plug S  10.0.1.9
removed  ttl=0      plug.local  Plug S  10.0.1.9
.RE
.fi
.PP
and
.B timers
prints one line per timer:
.PP
.nf
.RS
NAME                               STATE    DUE        RUNS  LAST RUN  TOOK
upkeep br0                         waiting  in 1m 33s  4     3.5s ago  210us
  re-announce router.local                  in 1m 33s
  expire      10.0.0.10                     in 3m 02s
.RE
.fi
.PP
With
.BR \-\-json ,
every reply is an object with an
.B ok
field.
A
.B watch
prints the events of the daemon's stream, each an object with
.BR event ,
.BR segment ,
.B ttl
and
.B device
(as in
.BR devices ),
and not the first reply.
Other successful replies carry
.BR segments ,
.B name
and
.BR devices ,
.BR probed ,
or
.BR timers ;
failures are
.BR {"ok":false,"error":"..."} .
.BR hostname ,
.BR target ,
.B address_ttl
and a service's
.B ttl
are
.B null
when unknown.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
The daemon answered with an error, for example an unknown segment name.
.TP
.B 2
Bad command line, or the daemon could not be reached.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /run/mdnsd/mdnsd.sock
Default control socket.
It is readable only by the owner and group of the daemon, so to use
.B mdnsctl
without privileges join the
.B mdnsd
group.
.SH EXAMPLES
List the segments, then the devices of one:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ mdnsctl segments
$ mdnsctl devices IoT
.fi
.RE
.PP
Look for devices on the IoT segment, then list what was found:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ mdnsctl probe IoT
$ mdnsctl devices IoT
.fi
.RE
.PP
Print every segment name, and every device address with its hostname:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ mdnsctl \-\-json segments | jq \-r '.segments[].name'
$ mdnsctl \-\-json devices media_stuff \e
    | jq \-r '.devices[] | "\e(.addresses[0]) \e(.hostname)"'
.fi
.RE
.PP
Find the Shelly plugs on the IoT segment, asking for them by type, and print
the address of each as it is found, asking again every minute:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ mdnsctl \-\-json devices \-\-query _shelly._tcp IoT \e
    | jq \-r '.devices[].addresses[0]'
$ mdnsctl \-\-json watch \-\-query _shelly._tcp \-\-interval 1min IoT \e
    | jq \-r \-\-unbuffered 'select(.event != "removed") | .device.addresses[0]'
.fi
.RE
.PP
Talk to the socket directly: one request per line, one line of JSON back, any
number of requests per connection:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ printf 'segments\endevices IoT\en' | ncat \-U /run/mdnsd/mdnsd.sock
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mdnsd (8),
.BR mdnsd.conf (5)
